A host reads a project's own .pi from a folder it names.

pi reads a trusted project's settings, extensions, packages, system
prompt and MCP servers from the project's .pi, which any session working
there can write. A host checks that code against what the person allowed
before each load, but pi then reads it again from the .pi, so code a
session writes between the check and the load runs unchecked. Settings
made from files now take the folder to read in the .pi's place, and a
host can name another before a reload: the package manager, the resource
loader and the MCP extension read the project from it, and an
extension's context says which folder it is. The host names a copy of
the .pi it checked, in a folder no session can write. With no folder
named, everything reads the project's .pi as before.

Apply only to the host's private pi build; ordinary interactive pi stays
unchanged.

